<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Du gamla, du fria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a> (published in Runa, <a href="/years/1845">1845</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Du gamla, du fria&quot; (&quot;You ancient, you free&quot;) was written in 1844 by the Swedish antiquarian Richard Dybeck under the title &quot;Sång till Norden&quot; (&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> to the North&quot;). He set it to a variant of the ballad &quot;Kärestans död&quot; (&quot;The Death of a Loved One&quot;), a medieval ballad type (D 280 in The Types of the Scandinavian Medieval Ballad) whose Västmanland variant had been recorded by Rosa Wretman at the beginning of the 1840s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Du gamla, du fria</b>&quot; (&quot;You ancient, you free&quot;) was written in 1844 by the Swedish antiquarian Richard Dybeck under the title &quot;Sång till Norden&quot; (&quot;Song to the North&quot;).</blockquote><a href="/songs/du-gamla-du-fria">Du gamla, du fria</a></li></ul></section>
